111
00:05:56.240 --> 00:06:00.760
So the objectives of this talk are threefold.

112
00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:05.960
I want to share what context is and why I think it's coming for you.

113
00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:09.040
It sounds very scary, but it's more like how it's going to benefit you.

114
00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:09.720
I would like to show

115
00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:13.520
you also some common pitfalls that we've looked into in the field.

116
00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:19.680
And finally, I want to share how — like how you should think.

117
00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:20.800
You want to

118
00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:24.560
enable your team to develop with context and how you can upskill your team now.

119
00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:28.760
First point: what is context and what is coming for you?

120
00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:31.280
I'm going to stand on the shoulders of giants here.

121
00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:34.080
I don't know if you recognize his face.

122
00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:36.000
So this is Patrick Debois.

123
00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:39.920
If you've ever heard of this term DevOps, he's the guy that coined the term.

124
00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:44.640
And Patrick has been, you know, at the front edge of DevOps.

125
00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:46.560
This is a handbook he wrote with Gene Kim.

126
00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:51.080
And something that he has been really thinking about is now this entire idea

127
00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:55.600
of the context development lifecycle. By the way, the really cool thing —

128
00:06:55.720 --> 00:06:58.520
Patrick works — I can say Patrick is my colleague.

129
00:06:58.520 --> 00:07:00.160
He's a really fantastic guy.

130
00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:04.680
So this is kind of the new sort

131
00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:08.640
of thinking he's having around the idea of context. So

132
00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:14.560
he advocates — and this is also what our startup believes in.

133
00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:18.360
At the core is the way software engineers are going to be developing

134
00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:21.640
in 2026: first you generate context.

135
00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:25.160
Context can be how libraries should be used, how

136
00:07:25.160 --> 00:07:28.320
you want to go about a certain process, what convention

137
00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:32.640
your small team should follow after generating that context.

138
00:07:32.680 --> 00:07:33.640
You want to —

139
00:07:33.640 --> 00:07:35.680
just like in good old-school

140
00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:37.880
test-driven development, you want to make sure this makes sense.

141
00:07:37.880 --> 00:07:40.480
This is tested in 2026.

142
00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:43.720
This is evaluations, or short term, it's evals.

143
00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:47.160
So how can you eval your context

144
00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:50.280
and how can you evaluate the output of an agent's context?

145
00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:53.320
I'll get a little bit more into detail.

146
00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:56.320
After you are happy with your context,

147
00:07:56.320 --> 00:07:57.760
you've evaluated it.

148
00:07:57.760 --> 00:08:01.640
You want to make sure that other teams are able to benefit from that.

149
00:08:01.640 --> 00:08:04.160
So this is now no longer just you and your small team.

150
00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:05.600
This is across departments.

151
00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:06.040
You know,

152
00:08:06.040 --> 00:08:07.240
these are the conventions

153
00:08:07.240 --> 00:08:11.160
we work with — I don't know — like, we had a talk with Neo4j.

154
00:08:11.560 --> 00:08:15.320
These are the conventions that we go by when we think about security practices

155
00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:16.320
at Neo4j.

156
00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:19.000
And then the last thing is the entire observation.

157
00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:22.000
This is where the lifecycle becomes a bit of a loop.

158
00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:25.160
Did the agent do what we want it to do?

159
00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:26.760
What did it misunderstand?

160
00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:30.240
What did it improvise, or when did it become silent?

161
00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:35.000
And how can we sort of improve that by regenerating that context. So.

162
00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:37.440
The three

163
00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:42.520
takeaways from this: the bottleneck has shifted from code to context.

164
00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:45.760
Context needs its own development lifecycle.

165
00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:48.960
And something interesting is like —

166
00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:52.080
infinite context won't save you.

167
00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:55.120
This is — this is kind of the key thing I like to hammer

168
00:08:55.120 --> 00:08:58.120
home.

169
00:09:00.520 --> 00:09:02.240
Context.

170
00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:03.440
Exactly.

171
00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:06.960
So on the team and enterprise

172
00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:12.240
section, we are thinking of this as like a flywheel.

173
00:09:12.280 --> 00:09:18.440
In fact, this is an image generated by Patrick.

174
00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:21.440
But I think it could also be called the context opportunity,

175
00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:24.440
which is: the context we develop

176
00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:28.040
on our own is then shared within our teams.

177
00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:32.000
And those at some point, depending on the scale of the enterprise,

178
00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:34.440
should also be shared cross-functionally.

179
00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:37.880
And at some point this becomes institutional wisdom.

180
00:09:37.880 --> 00:09:39.760
So — what does that mean?

181
00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:45.840
If there are specific API conventions within your organization,

182
00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:48.760
those can be defined first by an engineering leader.

183
00:09:48.760 --> 00:09:51.880
Once those are clearly explained, policies

184
00:09:51.880 --> 00:09:55.240
and conventions are agreed upon — business decisions have been made.

185
00:09:55.280 --> 00:09:58.240
Then you make sure that this context is evaluated.

186
00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:01.360
You evaluate as well how the agent is leveraging that context

187
00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:02.760
and what it outputs.

188
00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:06.360
You build scenarios around it to make sure that those are working,

189
00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:09.000
and then you can deploy those, and then you can govern those,

190
00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:11.600
and then you make sure the security is in place. So.

191
00:10:13.720 --> 00:10:16.280
To go a tiny bit deeper —

192
