462
00:24:41.400 --> 00:24:44.640
And so that becomes part of their CI.

463
00:24:44.760 --> 00:24:47.640
It just becomes part of their workflows.

464
00:24:47.640 --> 00:24:50.280
So if the skills are

465
00:24:50.280 --> 00:24:55.040
dependent on the model that you're using and that has been tested,

466
00:24:55.240 --> 00:24:58.360
we keep it like a catalog or metadata — or this:

467
00:24:58.600 --> 00:25:01.200
"this skill has been optimized under these conditions."

468
00:25:01.200 --> 00:25:04.400
And I mean, or even in the same markdown

469
00:25:04.400 --> 00:25:05.560
file, with some comments.

470
00:25:06.640 --> 00:25:08.800
Yeah, I would say two answers to that.

471
00:25:08.800 --> 00:25:11.440
The first one — this is why versioning is very important.

472
00:25:11.440 --> 00:25:17.120
And the second one is yeah, this is where also the governance of the skill,

473
00:25:17.160 --> 00:25:22.000
based on the convention, policy and practices of the team, matters.

474
00:25:22.040 --> 00:25:27.760
So if your team believes that our skill works well because of X, Y,

475
00:25:27.760 --> 00:25:33.320
Z — it's been evaluated and for some reason you'd like to use XYZ model —

476
00:25:33.520 --> 00:25:35.760
perhaps Haiku, because it's a smaller model,

477
00:25:35.760 --> 00:25:38.720
it will be cheaper and it works for this use case —

478
00:25:38.720 --> 00:25:41.160
then you have merit to continue doing it like this.

479
00:25:41.160 --> 00:25:45.400
But if another team within the organization has,

480
00:25:46.040 --> 00:25:48.600
for the sake of argument, a bigger budget, and they want to make sure they use

481
00:25:48.600 --> 00:25:53.240
