482
00:25:53.240 --> 00:25:55.480
and then they're going to have some sort of findings.

483
00:25:55.480 --> 00:25:56.840
Your skill works just as well.

484
00:25:56.840 --> 00:25:57.720
Or maybe actually,

485
00:25:57.720 --> 00:26:01.960
for this exact evaluation criteria for these scenarios that we care about,

486
00:26:02.000 --> 00:26:03.880
we want to actually leverage this different model.

487
00:26:03.880 --> 00:26:04.640
Well, actually, you know what?

488
00:26:04.640 --> 00:26:06.880
I'm just going to make a different skill.

489
00:26:06.880 --> 00:26:10.160
And so yours continues to run alongside for your process,

490
00:26:10.200 --> 00:26:12.800
while the other team is going to do that as well.

491
00:26:12.800 --> 00:26:15.800
It could be also a scenario where both of you are using the same skill,

492
00:26:15.960 --> 00:26:19.120
and just the different versions of the skills are outdated.

493
00:26:19.120 --> 00:26:24.720
And so I think the core takeaway here is:

494
00:26:25.280 --> 00:26:28.520
models are non-deterministic, and we are trying

495
00:26:28.520 --> 00:26:32.360
to figure out a way to bring a little bit of engineering back to it.

496
00:26:32.360 --> 00:26:36.400
And if we take anything from DevOps and test-driven development, it's that

497
00:26:36.400 --> 00:26:38.440
actually there is a path

498
00:26:38.440 --> 00:26:42.160
where you can evaluate what you feed and what you get out of it.

499
00:26:42.280 --> 00:26:44.760
I think that's — that's the key thing here.

500
00:26:44.760 --> 00:26:45.840
Does that make sense?

